---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = "/guides/list-growth";
const title = "SMS list growth: preserve the capture promise";
const desc = "Build a reachable audience with a clear reason to subscribe, separate channel permissions and a verifiable handoff to the sender.";
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} dateModified="2026-11-25" />
  <article class="container py-16 prose-shopify">
    <h1>SMS list growth: preserve the capture promise</h1>
    <p>Build a reachable audience with a clear reason to subscribe, separate channel permissions and a verifiable handoff to the sender.</p>
    <h2>Start with the promised message</h2>
    <p>A product alert, recurring promotion and order update are different expectations. Before building a form, write the brand, message purpose, intended frequency description and exit route the shopper sees. Keep the exact disclosure version alongside the capture source and timestamp. Review the wording for the markets and message categories involved; this operating register is not a legal approval.</p>
    <p>A waitlist can be useful without becoming a general promotional audience. Store the requested product or variant and the scope of the request. If the business wants a broader subscription, make that choice explicit rather than treating interest in one item as permission for every campaign.</p>
    <h2>Match the entry point to the record</h2>
    <table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th>Capture path</th><th>Evidence to retain</th><th>Failure to catch</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td>Storefront form</td><td>Rendered mobile and desktop disclosure, form version and channel choice</td><td>Phone submission silently enrolling an email-only shopper</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Shopify checkout</td><td>Actual checkbox state and destination subscription record</td><td>Shipping phone mistaken for marketing permission</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Keyword or QR promotion</td><td>Promotion artwork, destination and keyword mapping</td><td>Old signage connecting to a retired sender or broader promise</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Imported records</td><td>Original evidence, withdrawal history and mapping report</td><td>Import changing unknown or suppressed status to subscribed</td></tr>
    </tbody></table>
    <p>As concrete supplier examples, <a href="https://help.klaviyo.com/hc/en-us/articles/360056824732">Klaviyo documents a Shopify integration switch for checkout SMS subscribers</a>, while <a href="https://support.omnisend.com/en/articles/3529761-understand-sms-opt-in-methods">Omnisend documents several opt-in entry points</a>. Verify the route actually installed in your store. A supplier’s supported method does not prove that an old theme form still uses it.</p>
    <h2>Keep identity separate from permission</h2>
    <p>Normalize number format for matching and delivery, but do not let matching rewrite consent. A returning customer may have a different email address, a shared phone or an earlier withdrawal. Keep the original evidence when profiles merge and investigate conflicts before sending. A historical order is useful context, not a substitute for an SMS subscription.</p>
    <p>When a form requests both channels, retain the shopper’s choice for each. Withdrawing SMS permission should stop SMS marketing without silently changing an unrelated email preference; a broader withdrawal request needs its own handling. Route uncertain requests to the support owner instead of guessing from a generic “subscribed” label.</p>
    <h2>Proposed capture acceptance exercise</h2>
    <ol><li>Submit each live form on mobile and desktop with controlled new profiles. Save what was visible when the choice was made.</li><li>Repeat as a returning email-only customer and a previously suppressed SMS profile. Inspect both Shopify and the sending service.</li><li>Verify that the welcome content matches the capture promise and that the correct product identifier survives the handoff.</li><li>Withdraw permission while a welcome or alert is queued. Inspect suppression and the pending message before dispatch.</li><li>Exercise an invalid number, duplicate submission and failed connector. Confirm there is a visible failure record and an owner.</li></ol>
    <p>This is a proposed rehearsal, not an observed result. Do not use real shoppers as test recipients or reconstruct missing consent evidence from a successful delivery.</p>
    <h2>Deliver the promise without a second welcome</h2>
    <p>Inventory welcome messages in the form tool, native Shopify automation and external sender. Choose one owner for each message. If an alert must wait for a permitted local delivery window, recheck inventory, eligibility and expiry before release. A delayed welcome is not a reason to bypass the sending policy or add an unrelated promotion.</p>
    <p>Suppress the capture form where it no longer makes sense, such as immediately after a completed subscription. Keep a route for preferences and support. For expired waitlists, decide whether the request closes, remains open for the item, or needs a fresh invitation; do not silently broaden it.</p>
    <h2>Measure usable growth by source</h2>
    <p>Report unique new eligible subscribers by capture source and disclosure version. Separate form submissions, invalid records, duplicate identities, existing subscribers and unresolved evidence. Track early withdrawals, complaints and whether the promised alert was fulfilled. A large submission count can conceal an unusable list.</p>
    <p>For an acquisition cost worksheet, divide source spend by newly eligible unique subscribers under a defined observation period; report the numerator and exclusions. Assess incentives against contribution after product, fulfillment and messaging costs. This page supplies no expected signup rate or revenue lift. Review the source when early withdrawals rise rather than assuming the solution is a larger offer.</p>
    <p>Use <a href="/guides/consent">consent operations</a> for the permission record, <a href="/guides/quiet-hours">quiet hours</a> for dispatch, and <a href="/guides/email-sms-coordination">channel coordination</a> for message ownership. List growth owns acquisition integrity; those guides own the next decisions.</p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
